routes.brief:
First prerequisite: Prismatic cohomology: relative, absolute, Nygaard and log variants (PrismaticCohomology). Its PR.3–PR.5 plan the Nygaard filtration, Breuil–Kisin twists, Bhatt–Lurie's absolute prismatic cohomology and Hodge–Tate stacks, and PR.4 the syntomic complexes Z_p(i)(X) of arbitrary schemes with Bhatt–Lurie's étale comparison. This Part II adds Bhatt–Mathew's theory built on them: the Nygaard filtration on Hodge–Tate cohomology, F-smooth rings, the theorem that regular rings are F-smooth, and the identification of syntomic complexes of p-torsion-free F-smooth schemes with p-adic étale Tate twists.

Final theorems, as the paper states them, with the corrections in sourceIssues:
- Theorem 1.8. Let X be a p-torsion-free F-smooth scheme (e.g. regular and flat over Z) and i ≥ 0. Then Z/p^n(i)_X → τ^{≤i}Rj_*(μ_{p^n}^{⊗i}) is an isomorphism on cohomology sheaves in degrees < i. On H^i it is injective, with image the subsheaf generated by O_X^×-symbols; equivalently, the fibre square of p. 4 holds.
- Theorem 4.15. Regular noetherian rings are F-smooth, and p-complete noetherian F-smooth rings are regular.
- Corollary 4.20. Let R be a p-complete regular local ring with residue field k and R/pR F-finite, and let d = dim R + log_p[k:k^p]. Then WCart^HT_{Spf R} has cohomological dimension ≤ d, so Δ̄_R{i} ∈ D^{≤d}(Z_p). This is Bhatt–Lurie's Conjecture 10.1 under F-finiteness.
- Theorem 5.8. For X regular of finite type over a Dedekind domain with perfect residue fields at p, and semistable over the characteristic-p points, Sato's 𝔗_n(i)_X ≃ Z/p^n(i)_X canonically and multiplicatively. The paper's reduction uses R[1/p]^× = π^Z × R^×, which fails at points on several components of the special fibre (sourceIssues E6). Supply that case, or restrict the statement to where the special fibre is locally irreducible, and record which.
- Proposition 5.2. For A p-torsion-free, p-quasisyntomic and F-smooth, fib(F_p(i)(A) → RΓ_et(Spec A[1/p], F_p(i))) ∈ D^{≥i+1}.
- Proposition 5.5. For R strictly henselian local, (R^×)^{⊗i} → H^i(Z/p^n(i)(R)) is surjective.
- Theorem 5.1. For every qcqs derived scheme X, ⊕F_p(i)(X[1/p]) is the localization of ⊕F_p(i)(X) at v_1.

Cover every construction on the way, in this order.
(1) The elements of the prismatic cohomology of Z_p (§2):
- the bigraded rings E(α)⊗P(θ^{±1}) and E(α)⊗P(θ) for mod p Hodge–Tate and Nygaard cohomology;
- the perfectoid case (Example 2.5) and localization at θ (Proposition 2.6);
- v_1 ∈ H^0(F_p(p−1)(Z)) by flat descent from Z[ζ_{p^∞}] (Construction 2.7), with its direct prismatic construction (Remark 2.10);
- θ̃ and Proposition 2.9.
(2) The Nygaard filtration on Hodge–Tate cohomology (§3):
- N^{≥*}Δ̄_R, with gr ≅ ⊕N^iΔ_R/p and gr^0 ≅ R/p^{(−1)};
- detection of p by θ, and the twisted filtration on Δ̄_R{i}/p with graded pieces cofib(θ);
- relatively perfect maps of animated rings and base change (Definition 3.5, Remark 3.6, Proposition 3.7);
- weight gradings (Remarks 3.8 and 3.10, Construction 3.9);
- polynomial rings (Propositions 3.11 and 3.12, with equation (10) corrected, E2);
- quotients by a nonzerodivisor (Propositions 3.13–3.14, Corollaries 3.15–3.16).
(3) F-smoothness (§§4.1–4.3):
- Definitions 4.1 and 4.2, and Proposition 4.3;
- stability (Remarks 4.4–4.5, Propositions 4.6–4.10), citing Proposition 4.7 for Zariski and étale localization (E3);
- the Beilinson t-structure reformulation (Remark 4.11);
- the criterion over a perfectoid base (Proposition 4.12, after Bouis);
- Cartier smoothness (Definition 4.13, Proposition 4.14).
(4) Regular rings (§§4.4–4.5):
- Proposition 4.16 and Corollary 4.17;
- both directions of Theorem 4.15, via Proposition 4.19 and the fibre sequence (17);
- Corollary 4.20 and Lemma 4.21.
(5) Étale Tate twists (§5):
- Theorem 5.1, Lemma 5.3, and Propositions 5.2 and 5.4 (with the equalizer of weight i, E1);
- Propositions 5.5–5.7 and Theorem 1.8;
- Sato's twists and Theorem 5.8;
- Example 5.9 (special fibre X_k, E7).

Import, never re-plan:
- PrismaticCohomology PR.1–PR.5: the Hodge–Tate comparison; the Nygaard/conjugate comparison over perfectoids; Breuil–Kisin twists and the prismatic logarithm; Bhatt–Lurie's absolute prismatic cohomology, diffracted Hodge complex and Sen operator; Hodge–Tate stacks and WCart^HT_{A^d} = B(G_a^{♯d} ⋊ G_m^♯); syntomic complexes with flat descent, left Kan extension and the étale comparison.
- DerivedDeRhamCohomology DD.0, DD.2, DD.3, DD.5: the cotangent complex, derived exterior and divided powers, derived de Rham and Hodge completion, the conjugate filtration and Cartier isomorphism, the quasisyntomic site and p-complete Tor-amplitude.
- AInfCohomology AI.1: the décalage functor Lη.
- CrystallineCohomology CR.4: logarithmic de Rham–Witt sheaves. Take Sato's logarithmic Hodge–Witt sheaves on normal-crossing special fibres from CR.5 if it supplies them, and plan them here otherwise.
- PerfectoidQuotients Q3: André's lemma.
- Bloch–Kato's p-adic vanishing cycles, from the proposed Part II LocalFieldsPartIIKatoSwanConductors.
- MotivicEtaleKTheory M.5: Galois symbols and the Steinberg relation.
- The commutative algebra this paper routes to DeformationAndDerivedPatchingAlgebra R03.1/R03.3, DerivedDeRhamCohomology DD.0 and SchemeAndStackFoundations SF.0: Cohen structure, regular local rings, Avramov's and André's criteria, F-finiteness and Popescu.
- Étale cohomology from SchemeAndStackFoundations SF.2.
- Mathlib's IsRegularLocalRing and IsRegularRing.

Every definition gets functoriality, base change and at least three unit tests:
- A = Z_p: F-smooth, with Z/p(1) = μ_p and v_1, θ̃ explicit;
- F_p[t] and F_p(t): Cartier smooth; Corollary 4.20 with d = 1;
- a ramified O_K;
- Z_p[x]/(x^2): not regular, so not F-smooth;
- Z_p[x, y]/(xy − p) at the origin: regular and semistable, a point on two components, for Theorem 5.8;
- Z_p⟨t⟩ for Example 5.9.
